# Cochinita Pibil

Based on [https://www.cilantroparsley.com/cochinita-pibil-instant-pot/](https://www.cilantroparsley.com/cochinita-pibil-instant-pot/)

{ rating="0" image="None" }
:::
:::

## Ingredients

### Pork

- [ ] 3-4 pounds [pork shoulder]{ ing="pork-shoulder" }
- [ ] 4 oz [achiote paste]{ ing="achiote-paste" }
- [ ] 6 [oranges]{ ing="oranges" }, freshly squeezed (or 1 cup orange juice)
- [ ] 5 [limes]{ ing="limes" }, squeezed

### Optional Pickled Onion

- [ ] 1/2 [red onion]{ ing="red-onion" }, sliced
- [ ] 1/2 cup [water]{ ing="water" }
- [ ] 1/2 cup [white vinegar]{ ing="white-vinegar" }
- [ ] 1 tsp [coriander or cumin]{ ing="coriander-or-cumin" }
- [ ] 2 [bay leaves]{ ing="bay-leaves" }

### Garnish

- [ ] [Chopped cilantro]{ ing="chopped-cilantro" }
- [ ] [Lime wedges]{ ing="lime-wedges" }
- [ ] [Pickled onion]{ ing="pickled-onion" }

## Recipe

1. Make the sauce: Blend [achiote paste]{ ing="achiote-paste" }, [orange]{ ing="oranges" } juice, and [lime]{ ing="limes" } juice until smooth. Add to pork and marinate overnight (minimum one hour if time-limited).
1. Instant Pot method: Place all ingredients in Instant Pot and pressure cook on high for 1 hour.
1. Oven method: Preheat to 325°F. Line baking dish with three sheets aluminum foil. Add pork with marinade. Cover with three more foil sheets, seal well. Bake 3-4 hours.
1. Slow cooker method: Add all ingredients. Cook on high 3-4 hours or low 6-7 hours.
1. Shred the pork: Using a slotted spoon, transfer pork to bowl. Shred with two forks. Pour sauce back over meat.
1. Optional [pickled onions]{ ing="pickled-onion" }: Simmer [onion]{ ing="red-onion" }, [water]{ ing="water" }, [vinegar]{ ing="white-vinegar" }, coriander, and [bay leaves]{ ing="bay-leaves" } 2-3 minutes. Cool, refrigerate 1 hour.
1. Garnish: Top with [cilantro]{ ing="chopped-cilantro" }, [lime wedges]{ ing="lime-wedges" }, and/or [pickled onions]{ ing="pickled-onion" }.
1. Serve on rice, tortillas, or gorditas.

## Notes

- Don’t trim fat—it provides essential flavor
- Seal foil tightly when baking
- Tastes better the next day as flavors develop
